\begin{abstract}
Removing 5\% of vision tokens collapses SmolVLA from 59.2\% to 0.0\% success rate, while the same reduction on $\pi_{0.5}$ retains 98.2\% under importance-based selection but only 69.4\% under random selection. Token pruning transfers unpredictably across Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models because the vision connector governs token compressibility. We present an empirical study of this sensitivity along two axes: connector coupling (token-independent vs.\ token-entangling) and task complexity (single-step vs.\ multi-step manipulation). Token-entangling connectors exhibit task-amplified collapse: under random pruning at 90\% retention, X-VLA loses 1.4 percentage points on single-step tasks but 27.8 on multi-step tasks, a $20{\times}$ amplification. The coefficient of variation (CV) of post-connector token L2 norms provides a coarse compressibility indicator, partitioning models into uniform, graded, and catastrophic classes without downstream evaluation. For pixel-shuffle connectors where all hard deletion strategies yield 0.0\%, learned re-encoding recovers over 60\% success rate at 75\% compression regardless of module architecture, establishing deletion as the failure mechanism. Evaluation spans six VLA architectures (450M to 7B), three connector families, and four LIBERO task suites.
\end{abstract}

